    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "n = 2  ->  2   (1+1, 2)\nn = 3  ->  3   (1+1+1, 1+2, 2+1)\nn = 5  ->  8"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The pattern: 1D DP"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In **1D DP** the state is a single index — usually \"the answer for the first `i` items\" or \"for step `i`\". The recurrence looks back a fixed number of positions (one or two), so the table fills left to right and almost always shrinks to a couple of variables. This chapter goes from counting (Climbing Stairs) to minimising (Min Cost), to take-or-skip choices (House Robber), a circular twist (House Robber II), and a string version (Decode Ways)."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Think about the **last move**. To stand on step `i`, you came either from step `i − 1` (a 1-step) or from step `i − 2` (a 2-step). Those two groups of ways don't overlap and cover everything, so `ways[i] = ways[i − 1] + ways[i − 2]`. There's one way to be on step 0 (do nothing) and one way to reach step 1."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Plain recursion"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function climbStairs(n) {\n  if (n <= 1) return 1;                         // step 0 or 1: one way\n  return climbStairs(n - 1) + climbStairs(n - 2);   // last move was 1 step or 2 steps\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def climb_stairs(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 1                          # step 0 or 1: one way\n    return climb_stairs(n - 1) + climb_stairs(n - 2)   # last move was 1 step or 2 steps"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int climbStairs(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;                        // step 0 or 1: one way\n    return climbStairs(n - 1) + climbStairs(n - 2);   // last move was 1 step or 2 steps\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int climbStairs(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;                        // step 0 or 1: one way\n    return climbStairs(n - 1) + climbStairs(n - 2);   // last move was 1 step or 2 steps\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(2ⁿ), space O(n).** The same call tree as Fibonacci — n = 45 would take minutes."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. A table, filled left to right"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "climbing-stairs-table",
      "caption": "Each step's count is the sum of the two below it."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function climbStairs(n) {\n  const ways = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);     // ways[i]: ways to stand on step i\n  ways[0] = 1;\n  ways[1] = 1;\n  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n    ways[i] = ways[i - 1] + ways[i - 2];     // from one step below, or two\n  }\n  return ways[n];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def climb_stairs(n):\n    ways = [0] * (n + 1)                  # ways[i]: ways to stand on step i\n    ways[0] = ways[1] = 1\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        ways[i] = ways[i - 1] + ways[i - 2]   # from one step below, or two\n    return ways[n]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int climbStairs(int n) {\n    int[] ways = new int[n + 1];              // ways[i]: ways to stand on step i\n    ways[0] = 1;\n    ways[1] = 1;\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n        ways[i] = ways[i - 1] + ways[i - 2];  // from one step below, or two\n    }\n    return ways[n];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int climbStairs(int n) {\n    vector<int> ways(n + 1, 0);               // ways[i]: ways to stand on step i\n    ways[0] = 1;\n    ways[1] = 1;\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n        ways[i] = ways[i - 1] + ways[i - 2];  // from one step below, or two\n    }\n    return ways[n];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(n).**"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "3. Two variables"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function climbStairs(n) {\n  let twoBelow = 1, oneBelow = 1;            // ways[i - 2], ways[i - 1]\n  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) [twoBelow, oneBelow] = [oneBelow, twoBelow + oneBelow];\n  return oneBelow;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def climb_stairs(n):\n    two_below, one_below = 1, 1           # ways[i - 2], ways[i - 1]\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        two_below, one_below = one_below, two_below + one_below\n    return one_below"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int climbStairs(int n) {\n    int twoBelow = 1, oneBelow = 1;           // ways[i - 2], ways[i - 1]\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n        int cur = twoBelow + oneBelow;\n        twoBelow = oneBelow;\n        oneBelow = cur;\n    }\n    return oneBelow;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int climbStairs(int n) {\n    int twoBelow = 1, oneBelow = 1;           // ways[i - 2], ways[i - 1]\n    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {\n        int cur = twoBelow + oneBelow;\n        twoBelow = oneBelow;\n        oneBelow = cur;\n    }\n    return oneBelow;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(1).** Only the last two counts are ever read."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Plain recursion",
          "time": "O(2ⁿ)",
          "timeWhy": "Each call makes **two more**, re-solving the same steps like Fibonacci.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "The call stack goes **n** levels deep."
        },
        {
          "approach": "A table, filled left to right",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "Each of the **n** steps is computed **once** from the two before it.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "A table with **one entry per step**."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Two variables",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "The **same n steps**, one O(1) update each.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Only the **last two counts** are ever read."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**n = 1** — one way; the table must still have room for `ways[1]`.",
        "**n = 2** — two ways.",
        "**n = 45** — 1,836,311,903: still fits a 32-bit int; n = 46 wouldn't."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Steps of 1, 2 or 3** — add `ways[i − 3]`.",
        "**Any set of allowed steps** — sum `ways[i − s]` over every allowed step s (the Combination Sum IV recurrence).",
        "**Min Cost Climbing Stairs** — minimise a cost instead of counting. Next lesson."
      ]
    }
  ],
